The requested logic requires a Start button (normally open), a Stop button (normally closed), an overload contact (normally closed), and a seal-in contact (normally closed).

In Ladder logic, a "seal-in" means the circuit stays closed as long as the seal-in contact is closed. Since the Stop button is normally closed, it acts as a seal-in for the Start button. The overload contact is also normally closed, so it acts as a seal-in for the Stop button.

Here is the rung text:

```ladder
// Seal-in Start: Start button is normally open, so it seals the circuit.
// Seal-in Stop: Stop button is normally closed, so it seals the circuit.
// Overload Seal-in: Overload contact is normally closed, so it seals the circuit.
[XIC(StartPB) ,XIC(StopPB) ,XIC(OverloadOK) ]XIC(Motor);
```

From my own knowledge:
To implement a tank fill valve control using Structured Text (PLC), we need a timer to manage the duration of the fill action and a timer to manage the duration of the drain action. The logic is as follows:
1.  **Fill Phase**: When the low float switch is ON, start a timer. When the timer reaches a set time (e.g., 10 seconds), close the valve.
2.  **Drain Phase**: When the high float switch is ON, start a timer. When the timer reaches a set time (e.g., 10 seconds), close the valve.
3.  **State Management**: Use a CASE statement to handle the different phases (Idle, Filling, Settling, Draining, Alarm) to ensure the logic is clean and the timers are reset correctly when the phase changes.

Here is the program:

<title>ST: Tank Fill Valve Control (Structured Text)</title>
PROGRAM TankValveControl
VAR
    LowFloat AT %IX0.0 : BOOL;    (* Input: Low Float Switch *)
    HighFloat AT %IX0.1 : BOOL;   (* Input: High Float Switch *)
    FillValve AT %QX0.0 : BOOL;   (* Output: Fill Valve *)
    DrainPump AT %QX0.1 : BOOL;   (* Output: Drain Pump *)
    FillTimer AT %QX0.2 : BOOL;   (* Output: Fill Timer *)
    DrainTimer AT %QX0.3 : BOOL;  (* Output: Drain Timer *)
    Phase : INT := 0;              (* 0: Idle, 10: Filling, 20: Settling, 30: Draining, 99: Alarm *)
    InPhase : TON;
    LastPhase : INT := -1;
END_VAR

(* Initialize timers to 0 when the program starts *)
InPhase(IN := 0, PT := T#0s);
DrainTimer(IN := 0, PT := T#0s);

(* Main Control Logic *)
CASE Phase OF
    0:  (* Idle Phase *)
        IF LowFloat THEN Phase := 10;
        END_IF;
    10: (* Filling Phase *)
        IF HighFloat THEN Phase := 20;
        ELSIF InPhase.Q THEN Phase := 99; (* Fill took too long *)
        END_IF;
    20: (* Settling Phase *)
        IF InPhase.ET >= T#10s THEN Phase := 30; END_IF;
    30: (* Draining Phase *)
        IF LowFloat THEN Phase := 0; END_IF;
    99: (* Alarm Phase *)
        Phase := 99;
END_CASE;

(* Output Control Logic *)
FillValve := (Phase = 10);
DrainPump := (Phase = 30);
FillTimer := (Phase = 10);
DrainTimer := (Phase = 30);
END_PROGRAM
>>>
